        <p>Coroner H.C.istee ehnducted are inquiry on Saturday morning at the Cream Cottage Hospital, relative to the death of Thomem Yneall, 44 Yea. of age, • foe...n j iiner,who fell from a eceffniding on Satanday week, the 1125 Ind, while eager* at the new buildings at the Pollee Stratton, Roncorn.—Mr. A. ',either:l, aolicitot, represented the deceased man's relatives. Thom. Vonall, junior, eon of the deceased said he I resided et 31 Middlewich, Crewe, sod was a j•iiner's apprentice. liie father had been foreman joiner for Meads. Hulse, contractors, of Winalord, and had been lengerged at the new budditriss that wren being erected at Runcorn Police Station. On Saturdsy, the llth net, witness was working with Lie father in the early part of the morning. They were on • scaffold Minot 15ft high, and close to where his father watt Mending there woes window opening, the frainnwork of which had not been put in. At 10 they gea•leg work and hod breakfaet, I and when they commenced again at nine o'clock witness ' went to work in another hart .4 the building. A joiner named John ItOrelOn was i hen on the reef! Ad with wit- ness'. father. The ecalfold consisted of two up- righta, and they had nns plank to stand on. He believed the plank was loose. About 201 agnatel to ten o'clock withse beard a cry, and on looking down he law that his father had fallen through the window mooning Ile eurieted to carry him into the police etation, whore Im war seen by Or. McDougall, who adelaerl his removal to Crewe. Be was brought horn.. on an ambulance by the neat train, and witnem accompanied hint. When with/4a got to his father after the accident,he complained of his head, but on the way to Crewe he did notate anything .0 th how lit had occurred. Ho arrival et Cr.. he one taken home first and afterwards removed to the Cottage thee nitat. Winnow, had been working with his father at Runcorn for ahnot three monthe,and during that time he had never enthplained of gidilineer, but had always hot