      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-12-23_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>My Dux Winum.</p>
        <p>Pr is a very difficult matter indeed to find s heti that is picturesque, imitable to the censor, yet light on the head, and equipped for all emergencies. Light hats are very desirable,for the neuralgia and nervous headache, of the townswoman is nearly always traceable to the weight of the fashionable hat. In former flap', One felt pretty Kale in avoid• ing the picture hat and adopting a toque, which undoubtedly wee light. But now the toques ore.. heavy, if not heaver, than the large hate. The toque of the moment is a most elaborate arrange- ment of gathered velvet, plentifully wired, a pro- fuse clump of flowers, jewelled clasp, and either tips or a panache of more or lee, elaborate kind. Of course, every self-respecting toque toms up at the sole, usually sustained with a huge and heavy half bandeau.</p>
        <p>The hat over which I rejoice is not of felt, or of gathered velvet, or of chenille straw, or anything hot and heavy. It is of the new crinkled satin, which is quite the most woeful material that milli• ners employ. It hen the effect at • distance of • rich silk crepon, and is drawn or crinkled just in the name way that the fashionable millinery velvet is arranged. My hat in in thin crinkled material then, in a colt grey, and in a iashionable shape, curving away oil the face at one side. It is trimmed with white plumes up from the left side from under a bow of • suitable material or from under a buckle, or the lot might be trimmed with a hit of light ecru lone draping the brim below the buckle, but chows the hat shape at the edge over the foreheml. At the left there can be rosette. of lace and orange glace Here are a few hints on the choice of a few fancy dresees for evening wear and fen donning to attend some—for I presume you will go to some —of the entertainments amid social gatherings tab. ing place during the feetive season. A very hand. sonic drems isi,iails in white brocadeovith an inter- woven thread of gold ; and this, in view of the beauty of the febric, in made in simplest fashion, with tlm ski, quite plain and some mud point lace arranged iii tiehu fashion on the bodice, simplicity</p>
        <p>being under these circumstances decidedly anew:. tive. Another is • gown of blush rose pink crepe rhine, with insertions of yellowish lace encircling the long, eoftly hanging ekirt from waist to here. The Wake is low-cut and ruche. slightly over • long•endeil sash of rosy chitlon, WO also boasts of lace insertion as • trimming, while • drapery of the filmy fabric outlines the ofe,wint and forme the sleeves. Thin in one of the aspecte of the gown, the upect in which it will appear with distinction at particularly festive occasions; but should you want to transform your tall gown into a high•necked theatre or chimer drese, well, the means are ready to your hand. All that it is neeeeenry to do is to slip 9n • hal..</p>
        <p>sederbodloo, he rbap. MOTO COM, oboalisotto of polls elmfron Veiled kg, the rollarband being •ofteo.</p>
        <p>ly 1.44 in&amp; h Ihh Maly</p>
        <p>hang.ing trill of ,Inlfon, while the I. •, •Ik ..ve. of the etripe to Clone ace *lightly pi,t'..l at and then .lorted iion. e central hes,l id hoe applique. the lace frill,. which cf. duty them. ochres as aleeve, with the low both, •iil /lOW, with chasm:rig .doe. finial, the oh 'e pude of Lb. lone eleevee, while the lute tlke --one•tiine —dieollit• tint-dere the yoke of etattet and lon. In both t 0... he thew, to ,eliplitfully Folly and mart, end the beet that it to to pre eminently calculate.% to had 0 Anal exigence, to tloni it • decidedly good levee:moot from the c:•oziniioal point ol vten.</p>
