      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-12-23_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>CARTER'S LITTLE LIVER PILLS.</head>
        <p>CARTER'S LITTLE LIVER PILLS.</p>
        <p>I••4tfl*YlllM.(tfCtl r r beret ortmaNd ; •Ok tOf MIZOIJONT. i4r</p>
        <p>f OMEN'S</p>
        <p>Oi!Arlie House, and the adjacent grounda, cowering j some 800 acre., was purchased by Her Majesty in 1844, hut as the mansion was found to be totally insdi vete Ito the requirements of the Queen, her lin...hold, and • rapidly increasing family,it srns pulled down, and three menthe later her Majesty and Prince Albert laid the Brat atone of the present building. The Prince himself planned and des,gned the hnure,srhich took OW yearn to complete. Binge that time however,Oiborne flousewnd its environs, have been conaideratily enlarged. A now wing,the wonderful Indian Itnorn (upon the decoration of which no le. than £25.000 has been expendedi and the Private Chapel, or Prayer Rho, . it ts called, has been edded to the mansion it.elf, while the mem., and farms of Barton, vertuus cottages with their 'monde, end much wood lend,have Increased her Majwty's hold- ing in the Isle of White to 5,000 act.. The holler of freestone is of an Italian Idyle of archl rectum, judiciously modified to the exigencies of an Ennui..., climate. It la of pleasantly-varied elevation, and has many wings and faeadea, which lend to the building en air of light picturesqueness.. The tlonena entrance is list imposing than that at Windsor, and scarcely an picturesque.a that at Balmoral. It lies in a Itheltered nonk,and before it im • great granite-edge,' eir- cle,filled with heather from the Scrawl. memo. Here in this eeotful domain the Queen spends many happy hours. There are eight miles of dr,. on the seat, and the cottages—in reality delightful •illes—are eweu. pied by meanie. of the Royal •nd the Court. 'The bathing in 0-borne Bay in eacellent, and the pleas. ant shooting at Christmas time is adequate for Her Melody's gum.. The Queem is exceedingly fond of white heather, and when out driving in the Highlands al.e frequently atops to gather the beautiltil shrub. There is • dory to the effect that John Brown disputed or. one occadon with his Roy at mistress over a pie., she had limit picked. the QIIPPII thought it was the reel thing, hut John informed Fier Majesty in his customary bloat idyl.% that it was nothing of the kind. Welheck Abbey, the stately home of the Duke and Duch.% of Portland. which the Pete, and Prince. of Wales has lately honoured with a visit, is situated in the heart of what used to be Sherwood Forest, once the, trysting place of Friar Tuck. Robin Hood, and Majel Marian. The Abbey le splendid looking bodding.ibut the greet feat..gs of the estate are the undergrshund apartments built lw the Duke's eceentric Pilecesenr, whose horror of light, and the society of hi kind, is well known, and the hunting stablee, whir ie about • mibt. from the house and cover en acre of grimed. B dli the Duke and his wife era passionately leSnd And scarcely • day pawn hut that thet Dachees Hilda time to visit s few of her husband's equ7 favourite.. the Duelled. of Portland is extremely achinive. She has never shown the timeliest dedre cultivete trio genitasee,nr smart worldling.. titteisoaeol the few who keep up the tradition of wealth and rank, allied to respectebility,and she hes studiously *voided forming or cultivating • special social stet of her own. The Duke and Duchene have been, and are likely to remain very I popular, for they both make a point of spending their ' morels. fortune In their own country ,anit the Duchess has always been • trim fri-nd of both the frieh end Scotch cottage industries.</p>
        <p>... a.. . It as • mark of special favour, --hieM is very rarely I grantsd,for her MaJnety torte Lady Sybil Primrose, the 'defeat daughter of Lord Maebery, the privilege of • . private presentation. It will not be now nec ..... y for Lady Sybil to he presented at • drawing.roorn, hut elm will attend the first of the*, I tinctinnii, which ie held by the Queen, accompanied by her father. Although not pretty, Lady Sybil hoe con•iderable charra,and It is said she made a very favourable impression open her Majesty. Speaking of Lady Sybil Primmer% remit. ine of a 1 corn t bat ha. been told concerning the lat• Lady Moe... [wry, who, as everyone knows was a Rothschild, and ono of the richest women in the world. One day when walking scrim her drawing-p.m,, abe trinFed over the corner of • carpet She rang the bell and bid the butler to nail it down. Ile mid ha would wind the footman te see to it at once. The footman however, after inap. ot. jig the offending I ,or covering, went away to fetch the upper housemaid That denial evidently considered the nailing down of carpets as entirely coital&amp; her pro- vines, and sent for the under homemald, who in her turn requested the buttons to see to It. Finally, Lady Itneebery, who was an eminently practical avornan,nailid loon the carpet herself. The rush and Miele of the (-Airframe chopping in over for another year.. fact, the poor-tired kaiking unietanto are &amp;obi lem rejoicing over. Welly how their patience and strength last out,at this particular a...adios • matter little Mort of marvellous. Many women there ate, whn habitually go InN, shops without the fainh at idea of what they really wq, . i ... arid after giving an amount of trouble that would be alter n+ive even in acne. of es. pending many pound., lay out a few pence, or ourcharie nothieg,ned without one word of apology orander off bi freeh field, and pastures new. lie the any. have any of my reactant I wonder, ever watched • . mere men' doing hie Christmas sho