        <p>THIS 11118$1111.'</p>
        <p>It was very evident on Saturday evening that the Musical Society's repet mon or the venture in le, , ing a concert at popular prices WM much appreci- ated by the public, fur the l'arr !US was crowded to its fullest extent. "The Messiah" can usually be calculated upon to rime' at Christinantime, and when the musical attractions are combined with low charges, it is but natural that a large audience should assemble. On the whole the concert wise an undoubted success, though the music for the principal contralto might have been better interpreted. Miss Sarah Berry's singing was in many respects disappointing. Though posses- sing • rich and powerful voice ehe failed to do justice to 0 thou that tallest," and the preceding recitative was deficient in emphasis and declamation. The beautiful air' Ile was lee' pitied," met with more satisfactory treatment. and thongh Miss Berry may be said to hare scored • mincers here, even this numbir suffered from teo slow • footto. Of the singing of Miss Newcombe we can @peak only in high p though this young and gifted vocalist wait 1.0111•••hrit nervous to commence with. She, however,quickly rallied, and in interpreting the beautiful themes "Come unto, Him," "flow beautiful are the Met," and " I konw that my Redeemer Broth," she sang with • blares of erpronsion and depth of feeling which secured for her a well-deserved er•stim. The base portions of the oratorio were most capably and artistically interpreted by Mr. Arthur Barlow. His voice is not remarkable for strength or power, • shortcoming which is, however, more thau tom- palmated for lip ita purity of timbre and flexibility Mr. Barlow Is to be congratulated on the complete sum,ss attending his interpretations of the various solos falling to his lot in the oratorio in many of which he earned hearty dim by the energy and vigour ho Intoned into his singing. " Why do the Nations rage " was rendered with a spirit and wens which left nothing to be d while the numerous, powerful, recitatives and arim were hatchy lees successful in calling forth midence of rtie audience's appremation Mr. Maldwyn Humphreys sang the music for the principal tenor, in • tasteful and niuniciml y manner hot, he hardly made the bent of his opportunirlos in " Comfort Ye" Subnequeetly in " Thou shalt break them," he sang with appropriate ardour, and won loud plaudits. Turning to the chorus we Ind little to dispraise. Though on Ihe whole the mag- nificent cnorums ol the 'Mee/Sall" were not quite no finely sung as last year, precision in attack and a splendid volume of tone fully under control was still, as then a compactions feature One or two of the chequene were a trifle hurried and Mowed a tendency to run away in the tempo. This in of course often the ease in e All we like sheep " as in the preeent instance, but the fault is leas excusable in • chem. such aa " He trusted in Oet.." " For unto ue," •• Worthy the len:6," sail . Ilallelufsh," were specially praise- worthy for the massive effects of the fortissimo and the mahtle delicacy Of Many of the lotto Dec puma, Throughout the oratorio the performsnee of the orchestra was of • high order. The orsorture wee rendered with animation and preclidon, ant the ac- companiment-4 to the nunsorom aim were all played with a due regard to the position of the principal vocalist concerned. Mr J. B. Caldwell crecoded at the organyind Mr. F. If. Crossley wan of course at his accustomed place an conductor.</p>
