        <p>WAKRINOTI)N AND TILE ENGINEERS' DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have only one remark to make in reference to the reply of Ow Trade. Council to • letter of mine which you were good enough to publish some weeko ago, deal- Wg with the engineers dispute. What I then suggested was that such a representative body as the Trades Coon- oil should .irganise a local defence fund on behalf 44 the engineers and their losked.out workmen, and appeal to the general putilio—not exactly t • trade umonista,hut to those uncomiected with trade organisations, amongst Whom there inunt be many sympathisers,f seandance. This op, oars to have been conertiod tV those co, nectod with the Trade. Council as an appeal for help from the Trade. Council itself. Bow this till come about I fail to underatand.—Vour• etc., December 21M, 1697.</p>
        <p>WARKINGIfONIAN.</p>
        <p>THE EARLEiCIOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY, LIMITED.—NONiCONFORMITY WITH THE 1&amp;W. Sir.—l rbeeree the prosecution in the shone case was Conducted on behalf of the Royal Lancashire Agricul- tural Society in conjunction with the County Council, and have no doubt their active intereet in thew matter. is intended tribe public griod. But when their inspec- tors meet with a slight infringement ot the law, and clearly we no indication of haul, nor the existence of any twitter to do so, it smack. Wein&amp; of red tape to take legal proceedings. Although the Society was at fault in ant complying with the full letter of the hier it waft guilty of no moral wrong on one Could be deceived situ the nature el the IIiILaMICP, the , word,”ldargerine"being diatinctly printed on the pane, Wrapper. redwing each separate lb.,aad care war taken to supply it only when requested by the purchaser. 'The obeervance if this practice during the last eight years had the effect of reducing its sale to a very small quantity Indeed. compared with butter sold. 4 rs we uphold the in Biction itt halZ'oPeesorantoall who defraud the public either by the malt of margarine for butter or in any other way. Our t Renee wet one of a paltry character. No public advantage followed the conviction, nor could Ins prosecutors claim any satisfaction from the case and if their inspectors were instructed to metre themselyee ac- quainted with comparative methods of business it would avoid anodise Rimier actions.—Thanking you in antici- pation. I am, air, on behalf of the eociete, D. VORRES MR, President.</p>
